{"id":"baseline-predictor","t":"Baseline predictor with regularised biases","tags":["Recommender Systems","Regression"],"d":"Medium","rt":"numpy","fn":"fit_baseline","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"Fit the baseline $\\hat r_{ui} = \\mu + b_u + b_i$ the way Koren describes, with two shrunk averages.\n\nWrite `fit_baseline(ratings, n_users, n_items, lam_i, lam_u)` for `(u, i, r)` triples:\n\n1. $\\mu$ = mean of all ratings.\n2. $b_i = \\dfrac{\\sum_{u \\in R(i)} (r_{ui} - \\mu)}{\\lambda_i + |R(i)|}$.\n3. $b_u = \\dfrac{\\sum_{i \\in R(u)} (r_{ui} - \\mu - b_i)}{\\lambda_u + |R(u)|}$.\n\nUsers or items with no ratings get bias 0. Return `(mu, bu, bi)` with `bu` of length `n_users` and `bi` of length `n_items`.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef fit_baseline(ratings, n_users, n_items, lam_i, lam_u):\n    \"\"\"Return (mu, bu, bi).\"\"\"\n    mu = float(np.mean([r for _, _, r in ratings]))\n    return mu, np.zeros(n_users), np.zeros(n_items)\n","hints":["Item biases come first; user biases are computed on residuals after removing mu and b_i.","np.bincount(idx, weights, minlength) sums weights per index, which gives every numerator in one call.","The lambda in the denominator pulls biases toward 0 for items with few ratings."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef fit_baseline(ratings, n_users, n_items, lam_i, lam_u):\n    u = np.array([x[0] for x in ratings]); i = np.array([x[1] for x in ratings])\n    r = np.array([x[2] for x in ratings], float)\n    mu = float(r.mean())\n    bi = np.bincount(i, r - mu, n_items) / (lam_i + np.bincount(i, minlength=n_items))\n    bu = np.bincount(u, r - mu - bi[i], n_users) / (lam_u + np.bincount(u, minlength=n_users))\n    return mu, bu, bi\n","ex_md":"## Why a baseline\nBefore any factor model, the global mean plus user and item offsets already explains a large share of rating variance. It is the floor every recommender should beat, and it is the starting point for the bias terms in matrix factorisation.\n\n## Why shrinkage\nAn item with one 5-star rating would otherwise get $b_i = 5 - \\mu$. Dividing by $\\lambda + n$ treats $\\lambda$ as pseudo-ratings at the global mean, so sparse items stay near zero until evidence accumulates. Koren used $\\lambda_i = 25$ and $\\lambda_u = 10$ on Netflix data.\n\n## Order matters\nComputing $b_u$ on residuals after $b_i$ avoids double counting: a user who watches only popular films is not mistaken for a generous rater.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- How would you add a time-dependent item bias for films whose popularity drifts?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"[(0, 0, 5.0), (1, 0, 3.0), (1, 1, 1.0)]\n2\n2\n0.0\n0.0","out":"(3.0, array([ 1. , -0.5]), array([ 1., -2.]))"},{"in":"[(0, 0, 5.0), (1, 0, 3.0), (1, 1, 1.0)]\n2\n3\n25.0\n10.0","out":"(3.0, array([ 0.17508418, -0.16642925]), array([ 0.07407407, -0.07692308,  0.        ]))"}],"lessons":[],"related":["matrix-factorization-sgd-step"],"theory":[],"ntests":5,"tj":"[{\"a\": [[{\"__tup__\": [0, 0, 5.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [1, 0, 3.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [1, 1, 1.0]}], 2, 2, 0.0, 0.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [3.0, {\"__nd__\": [1.0, -0.5], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [1.0, -2.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [[{\"__tup__\": [0, 0, 5.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [1, 0, 3.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [1, 1, 1.0]}], 2, 3, 25.0, 10.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [3.0, {\"__nd__\": [0.1750841750841751, -0.1664292497625831], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.07407407407407407, -0.07692307692307693, 0.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}]}}, {\"a\": [[{\"__tup__\": [2, 3, 4.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [4, 0, 1.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [4, 5, 2.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [1, 5, 3.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [1, 4, 2.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [2, 3, 3.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [0, 0, 5.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [3, 5, 3.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [4, 1, 3.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [3, 0, 2.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [0, 2, 5.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [0, 2, 3.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [4, 1, 3.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [1, 0, 4.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [0, 1, 3.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [2, 0, 5.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [3, 5, 1.0]}, {\"__tup__\": [3, 1, 3.0